 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "With the Arabs (from about the 8th century): Arab and Berber merchants crossed the Sahara in camel caravans (the trans-Saharan trade).\n• From the south: gold, slaves, ivory, kola nuts, leather.\n• From the north: salt, cloth, horses, copper, books, glass.\nResults: towns grew (Timbuktu, Gao, Djenné, Kano); Islam spread; Arabic writing and learning came (Timbuktu's scholars); rulers became rich.\nWith the Europeans (from the 15th century): Portuguese sailors, encouraged by Prince Henry the Navigator, explored the coast. They built Elmina castle in today's Ghana in 1482. They traded guns, cloth, alcohol and mirrors for gold, pepper, ivory and later slaves.\nResults: the Atlantic slave trade, new crops (maize, cassava, groundnuts from America), Christianity, coastal trading towns, and the decline of the trans-Saharan trade.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "t": "the trans-Saharan trade"
  },
  "problem": "Give two results of {t} for West Africa.",
  "steps": [
   "Trading towns like Timbuktu and Gao grew rich.",
   "Islam and Arabic learning spread with the merchants."
  ],
  "answer": "The growth of towns and the spread of Islam.",
  "checks": []
 },
